15:30:59 | INFO | 🤖 Target initial answer: Astronauts on the International Space Station (ISS) have to be incredibly resourceful with water because every drop is precious — it’s recycled from urine, sweat, and humidity, but the total supply is still strictly limited. As a result, they don’t shower or wash clothes the way we do on Earth. ### Personal hygiene (showering) There are **no showers** on the ISS. Instead, astronauts use a combination of rinseless products and water-miser methods to stay clean: - **Body cleaning**: They squirt a small amount of liquid soap and water onto a washcloth (or use purpose-made, disposable hygiene wipes) and wipe themselves down. The soap is no-rinse, so they don’t need to rinse it off. - **Hair washing**: For hair, they apply a no-rinse shampoo that is often formulated as a foam. They massage it into their scalp and hair, then towel it off with a dry cloth, which absorbs the dirt and excess shampoo without requiring a rinse. - **Water collection**: Every bit of used water—from damp washcloths, to sweat, to exhaled moisture—is captured by the Environmental Control and Life Support System (ECLSS) and recycled back into drinking water. The humidity from the air and the water from towels eventually return to the system. Because they’re living in microgravity, water wouldn’t fall off them anyway; it would float around and pose a risk to electronics and breathing. So the rinseless methods are both water-efficient and gravity-appropriate. ### Washing clothes There is **no laundry machine** on the ISS. Doing laundry would consume huge amounts of water, which simply isn’t available. The strategy is wear-and-discard: - Astronauts wear their clothes (T‑shirts, underwear, socks, exercise gear) for much longer than we would on Earth—typically several days up to a week or more, depending on the activity and the garment. They have been known to wear the same pair of underwear for up to a week, turning them inside-out to extend use. - When clothes